          <figcaption>The dashed edge is the part that matters. A local run or a review finding sends the work back to implementation; it is not a one-way pipeline.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <details class="build-text-version">
          <summary>Text version of the workflow</summary>
          <ol>
            <li>Start with the outcome and limits. Use Get Ready only when the environment or target is not already verified.</li>
            <li>Turn the brief into an Init specification. The spec is portable data; it grants no execution authority.</li>
            <li>The agent implements the change in the selected source, with tests and a local run.</li>
            <li>Review the rendered phone, desktop, keyboard and failure states. Send findings back to implementation.</li>
            <li>Release through the guarded path, then verify the exact source, hostname and behaviour. The next change starts a new loop.</li>
          </ol>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="limits">
        <h2>What this proves, and what it does not</h2>
        <div class="build-limits">
          <div><h3>Proved in this run</h3><ul>
            <li>The exact-source invitation contract works against two real local Workers and real SQLite-backed D1 behaviour.</li>
            <li>A pending note is private; approval makes it public; featuring is a separate explicit card; hiding returns 404 for the item API and media and removes the card.</li>
            <li>Closed, paused, full and unavailable states do not render a send action, and mounted/standalone links preserve their destinations.</li>
            <li>The owner can link, replace or stop the invitation from the native collection control; a hidden or pending book is refused.</li>
            <li>The article, screenshots and diagrams are built from the same repository and capture run that are linked here.</li>
          </ul></div>
          <div><h3>Not proved</h3><ul>
            <li>No hosted deployment of this contribution feature is claimed from the local run, and the owner's live profile configuration was not changed.</li>
            <li>No photo upload from the Linkarooie CTA, no public comment thread, no replies, notifications, guest accounts or general multi-tenant signup.</li>
            <li>No screen-reader, physical touch-device, external social unfurl or full migration acceptance.</li>
            <li>Docker was not exercised for this walkthrough; the supported local path is Wrangler's runtime.</li>
            <li>Restoring code does not restore hidden or deleted data. Configuration rollback and content/media recovery remain separate.</li>
          </ul></div>
        </div>
        <p>The guest code is an existing narrow admission control, shared by the owner. It is not an account, and it never grants the owner studio. If the owner has not shared it, the form explains that before asking for a message.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2>Try it, or take the source</h2>
        <p>The starting point is <a href="/init">Init</a> for a portable composition, <a href="/ready">Get Ready</a> when the environment is not yet proven, and <a href="/manage">Your app, your admin</a> for the owner surface. The public fictional stacks remain available at <a href="/s/fictional-trail-notes">Fictional Trail Notes</a> and <a href="/s/fictional-launch-board">Fictional Launch Board</a>.</p>
